**Synchronization and mutexes.** When two threads update shared state, interleaving can cause race conditions. A mutex lets only one thread hold the lock and run the critical section. Java offers:

- `synchronized` method or block: uses the object's intrinsic monitor. A `static synchronized` method locks the `Class` object. It guarantees mutual exclusion and visibility (happens-before) and releases the lock automatically, even on exception.

        class Counter {
            private int c;
            public synchronized void inc() { c++; }
        }

- `ReentrantLock`: explicit `lock()` / `unlock()` (always in `finally`), plus `tryLock`, timeouts, interruptible locking and fairness. Reentrant means the same thread can re-acquire it.
- A `Semaphore(1)` works as a non-reentrant mutex; `AtomicInteger` and `ConcurrentHashMap` avoid explicit locks for simple cases; `volatile` gives visibility but not atomicity.
- Dangers: deadlock (inconsistent lock ordering), holding locks too long, and contention. Keep critical sections small.

**JUnit.** The standard Java unit-testing framework. Tests are methods annotated `@Test`, with setup and teardown (`@BeforeEach`, `@AfterEach`) and assertions such as `assertEquals`. Pair it with a mocking library (Mockito) to isolate units. Good tests are fast, independent and deterministic.

**Selenium.** Drives a real browser (via WebDriver) to automate end-to-end UI tests: find elements, click, type, assert page state. These tests are slower and more brittle than unit tests, so they sit at the top of the test pyramid.

**CI/CD scripting.** A pipeline (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI) builds the code, runs unit and UI tests, and deploys on success. Pipelines are defined as code in YAML or a Jenkinsfile (Groovy), with shell or Python scripts for steps.

If you do not know Java well, say so, then reason from the concept: "I know the idea of a mutex from OS; in Java I believe the keyword is `synchronized`".
